[{:seon.task/name "a-send-parks-like-a-receive"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by the blocking-calls audit of 2026-10-25 (claude_scratch/archive/blocking-calls-2026-10-25.md; rows 5, 6, 28). What exists: seon.sock/recv! parks on a main carrier (MSG_DONTWAIT, then poller/wait-ready, sock.clj:195-203) but the sends do not: write-all! (sock.clj:230-238) calls lib/c-write on a blocking socket and c-sendmsg (sock.clj:25, :304, fd passing) is :blocking, so every message to root from the agent, the test child and the fork server (fork_server.clj run-loop send!) holds the carrier, and with it every other fiber, while root is slow to drain a full socket buffer. Correct, at the one edge: send with MSG_DONTWAIT on a fiber of a main carrier, on EAGAIN park on poller/wait-ready fd :write and retry the rest; a short send continues from the offset; sendmsg the same. Linux needs MSG_NOSIGNAL (no SO_NOSIGPIPE). Wiki owed in docs/flow.md in the same commit: in a process that runs only on the main carrier a foreign call that waits on another process or the network must park (nonblocking fd plus wait-ready) or be bounded by its own work; :blocking only makes the call collect-safe. Proof to write: a sender to a peer that does not read keeps its process answering its other fibers until the peer drains."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "a-frame-is-written-whole"
  :seon.task/plan "Found live 2026-10-28 (the 0.7 confirm; claude_scratch/hang-2026-10-28-a1-prove/README.md, class A cause): agent a2's ~3 MB :tests answer failed whole in the kernel (its socket counted 5,176,484 bytes sent, root's end had 2,164,731 delivered: one frame missing), seon.sock/write-all! read the errno as :epipe (it answers :epipe for every negative write but EINTR), and seon.agent/await-op! sends handle!'s answers with doseq, dropping send!'s :dead. So the agent believed it answered and root's api held the :prove and :tests asks forever. Shown directly: send! to a live peer through a full non-blocking socket answered :epipe after 65,536 of 3,000,046 bytes. Correct, at the one edge (seon.sock/write-all! and send-frame!'s sendmsg): EINTR retries; EAGAIN and ENOBUFS (the kernel cannot take the bytes now) wait until the socket can take them (poll POLLOUT, a declared blocking call: no fiber switch mid-frame, so two fibers' frames never interleave; parking is task seon.sock/a-send-parks-like-a-receive, which needs one writer per socket first) and go on from the offset; EPIPE and ECONNRESET are :epipe (every peer's death also has its own end: the reader's EOF, NOTE_EXIT); any other errno throws :seon.sock/write-failed with :errno, :fd, :sent and :of from the edge, so no caller can drop it. seon.agent/send! ends the agent at :epipe (root is gone, as a :dead read already does), and its callers' (= :dead ...) checks go. docs/failure-points.md's write row states it."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.sock-test/a-frame-larger-than-the-socket-buffer-reaches-a-slow-live-peer-whole seon.sock-test/a-write-the-kernel-refuses-names-its-errno seon.sock-test/a-write-to-a-gone-peer-is-epipe}}]
